<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Runs scripts/cctv-daily-kr.sh once a day on a Mac that sits in Korea.

  Install:
    cp scripts/launchd/kr.247streams.cctv-daily.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    # edit the two paths below to match where the repo actually is
    launchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/kr.247streams.cctv-daily.plist 2>/dev/null
    launchctl load  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/kr.247streams.cctv-daily.plist
    launchctl start kr.247streams.cctv-daily     # one run now, to prove it works

  05:12, not 05:00: the whole world's cron jobs fire on the hour, and this one
  waits on a government API that is slowest exactly then.

  If the Mac is asleep at 05:12 the job does not vanish — launchd runs it at
  the next wake. A day that is missed entirely is a day of dead addresses, so
  the honest upgrade is a small Korean VPS that is never asleep.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>kr.247streams.cctv-daily</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>-lc</string>
    <!-- Through a login shell on purpose: launchd starts jobs with almost no
         PATH, and this needs python3, curl and ssh. -->
    <string>exec "$HOME/Louver-LIVE/scripts/cctv-daily-kr.sh"</string>
  </array>

  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
  <dict>
    <key>Hour</key><integer>5</integer>
    <key>Minute</key><integer>12</integer>
  </dict>

  <!-- Both streams in one file, appended, so a failed day can be read after
       the fact. The script timestamps every line it prints. -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/tmp/cctv-daily-kr.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/tmp/cctv-daily-kr.log</string>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
